1
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:14,240
Greetings and welcome to this introductory module on BioRisk Management.

2
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:20,240
BioRisk management encompasses both biosafety management and biosecurity management.

3
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:26,240
In this introductory module, I will be introducing you to the fundamental differences

4
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:30,240
between biosafety management and biosecurity management.

5
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:37,240
I will also introduce you to some of the key terminologies used in the following lectures.

6
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:44,240
Biosafety management pertains to the unintentional release of a biological agent.

7
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:52,240
Unintentional release can be in the form of a breach of containment from a laboratory facility.

8
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:57,240
This release is not intended. However, it has consequences

9
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:01,240
and can pose a significant threat to public health and safety.

10
00:01:03,870 --> 00:01:08,870
Biosafety management, on the other hand, focuses on the intended release of a biological agent.

11
00:01:08,870 --> 00:01:15,870
This concerns bioterrorism. A person with a malicious intent

12
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:19,870
can steal a biological agent from a laboratory

13
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:25,870
and use it to inflict a significant harm on public health and safety.

14
00:01:25,870 --> 00:01:29,870
This is what differentiates biosafety and biosecurity.

15
00:01:29,870 --> 00:01:33,870
It is purely the intent of the end user.

16
00:01:33,870 --> 00:01:38,870
We now move on to another key concept which is containment.

17
00:01:38,870 --> 00:01:47,870
Containment basically defines the limits of operation of a biological safety facility.

18
00:01:47,870 --> 00:01:54,870
In biological agents such as a virus or a bacteria must be contained within a facility.

19
00:01:55,870 --> 00:02:01,870
In order to prevent its escape and the consequent harm to public health and safety.

20
00:02:01,870 --> 00:02:08,870
There are two basic kinds of containment. These are primary containment and secondary containment.

21
00:02:08,870 --> 00:02:14,870
Primary containment pertains to the protection of the laboratory personnel

22
00:02:14,870 --> 00:02:19,870
from the biological agent by the application of pertinent controls.

23
00:02:19,870 --> 00:02:24,870
Secondary containment, on the other hand, pertains to the protection of the environment

24
00:02:24,870 --> 00:02:30,870
associated with a particular facility from the biological agent

25
00:02:30,870 --> 00:02:34,870
by the application of pertinent controls.

26
00:02:34,870 --> 00:02:40,870
Both primary and secondary containment must be applied concurrently

27
00:02:40,870 --> 00:02:45,870
in order to manage a biological agent effectively.

28
00:02:45,870 --> 00:02:50,860
What exactly are biological agents?

29
00:02:50,860 --> 00:02:59,860
Biological agents such as bacteria, viruses, fungi, prions and other subviral particles

30
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:06,860
which pose a significant threat to public health and safety are classified as biological agents.

31
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:10,860
These biological agents are further defined into risk groups.

32
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:14,860
There are four risk groups as defined by the World Health Organization.

33
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:20,200
In order to assess the risk posed by a biological agent,

34
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:28,200
a thorough biological management system involves the application of a risk management protocol.

35
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:36,740
We always commence biological safety management with a risk assessment.

36
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:42,740
Following a risk assessment, we put into place the appropriate controls

37
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:46,740
to mitigate or reduce the risk posed by a biological agent.

38
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:52,740
Finally, we assess the performance of the controls by auditing our performance.

39
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:59,740
These three steps form the basis for bio risk management.

40
00:03:59,740 --> 00:04:05,740
We commence with risk assessment, then we move on to risk mitigation

41
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:09,740
and finally we end with performance assessment.

42
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:13,740
This is a cyclical process which is continuously improved

43
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:19,740
in order to mitigate risks posed when working with biological agents.

44
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:31,460
Let us look at risk mitigation, risk assessment and performance assessment in separate topics.

45
00:04:31,460 --> 00:04:39,460
Risk assessment basically implies the usage of a risk matrix

46
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:45,460
to identify the risk posed by working with a specific biological agent.

47
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:50,460
Risk is measured in terms of likelihood and consequence.

48
00:04:50,460 --> 00:04:58,460
For instance, a laboratory may host a biological agent in a refrigerator.

49
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:03,460
It does not pose any risk when it sits inside the refrigerator.

50
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:09,460
However, when a laboratory technician begins to process this sample

51
00:05:09,460 --> 00:05:15,460
using different laboratory processes such as DNA extraction, protein extraction, PCR or ELISA,

52
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:18,460
the risk level increases.

53
00:05:18,460 --> 00:05:24,460
Risk is always measured in terms of the likelihood and consequence.

54
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:29,460
We measure the likelihood in terms of the likelihood of the breach of containment

55
00:05:30,460 --> 00:05:36,460
and we measure the consequence in terms of the impact on the laboratory user and the environment.

56
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:40,460
When we have assessed the risk posed by a biological agent

57
00:05:40,460 --> 00:05:48,460
and the associated operations thoroughly, we move on to the next step which is risk mitigation.

58
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:55,460
Risk mitigation relies on the application of the five basic controls.

59
00:05:55,460 --> 00:06:03,460
We have five controls. These are elimination, substitution, engineering controls,

60
00:06:03,460 --> 00:06:07,460
administrative controls and personal protective equipment.

61
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:15,460
Controls are used in conjunction with each other in order to mitigate the risk posed by a biological agent.

62
00:06:15,460 --> 00:06:23,460
Elimination is basically the process of not performing any operation with a biological agent

63
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:30,460
because the risk posed by that particular agent is too high or if you do not have an appropriate facility.

64
00:06:30,460 --> 00:06:36,460
Substitution basically focuses on weakening the pathogen.

65
00:06:36,460 --> 00:06:43,460
For instance, viruses can be attenuated or weakened so as to make them more manageable in a laboratory setting

66
00:06:43,460 --> 00:06:48,460
without posing a significant risk to the laboratory operators and to the environment.

67
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:57,460
Engineering controls are specific facilities which are designed to contain a biological agent

68
00:06:57,460 --> 00:07:03,460
and offer a safe working environment to the laboratory users.

69
00:07:03,460 --> 00:07:08,460
Administrative controls pertain to the standard operating procedures

70
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:12,460
and other training which is provided to a laboratory user

71
00:07:12,460 --> 00:07:18,460
in order to ensure a safe and conducive working environment

72
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:23,460
and in order to ensure the containment of that particular biological agent.

73
00:07:23,460 --> 00:07:31,460
And you have the final level of mitigation which is the personal protective equipment.

74
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:37,460
I am currently wearing a biosafety suit which offers me protection against the biological agent.

75
00:07:37,460 --> 00:07:45,460
This will basically prevent my skin from contact with a biological agent.

76
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:52,460
However, if the biological agent is transmitted by aerosols, there is a risk of inhalation.

77
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:57,460
In this case, I have to use additional personal protective equipment

78
00:07:57,460 --> 00:08:05,460
such as masks or eyewear in order to prevent inhalation of that particular biological agent.

79
00:08:05,460 --> 00:08:11,460
Most of the respiratory viruses are transmitted via inhalation.

80
00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:17,460
In this case, one needs to use a proper risk mitigation measure

81
00:08:17,460 --> 00:08:24,460
such as a mask or a powered air purified respirator to prevent risk of exposure to that biological agent.

82
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:30,460
Some biological agents may also be transmitted via the ocular route through the eyes

83
00:08:30,460 --> 00:08:39,460
in which case one needs to use proper eyewear in order to protect oneself from this particular biological agent.

84
00:08:39,460 --> 00:08:44,460
So these are the five basic controls elimination, substitution, engineering controls,

85
00:08:44,460 --> 00:08:48,460
administrative controls and personal protective equipment.

86
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:55,460
Once we have finished our risk mitigation, we look at the residual risk

87
00:08:55,460 --> 00:08:59,460
or the risk after application of all these controls.

88
00:08:59,460 --> 00:09:05,460
The laboratory users and the laboratory management must then decide whether this risk,

89
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:14,460
which is termed as residual risk, is sufficiently addressed in order to work in that particular facility.

90
00:09:14,460 --> 00:09:19,460
Please take note that residual risk may vary from facility to facility

91
00:09:19,460 --> 00:09:27,460
and you will have to refer to your own unique situations in order to address this residual risk.

92
00:09:27,460 --> 00:09:35,060
We now move on to the third step in Biorisk Management, which is performance assessment.

93
00:09:35,060 --> 00:09:42,060
Performance assessment basically involves auditing or looking back at your records

94
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:45,060
to determine whether there was any breach of containment.

95
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:51,060
If you did observe any breach of containment in the form of an accident or an incident,

96
00:09:51,060 --> 00:09:57,060
you must go back and look at your standard operating procedures and modify them accordingly.

97
00:09:57,060 --> 00:10:03,060
Please take note that documentation is critical to every biosafety operation.

98
00:10:03,060 --> 00:10:11,060
One needs to maintain appropriate documentation and all documentation must be controlled

99
00:10:11,060 --> 00:10:20,060
and traced in order to identify potential breaches of containment at a biosafety facility.

100
00:10:21,060 --> 00:10:27,060
So, that basically covers the key aspects of Biorisk Management.

101
00:10:27,060 --> 00:10:36,060
We have risk assessment, risk mitigation by the application of the five controls and performance assessment.

102
00:10:36,060 --> 00:10:44,060
I hope this introductory video has clarified some of the terminologies associated with Biorisk Management.

103
00:10:44,060 --> 00:10:49,060
I will discuss these further in my forthcoming lecture notes.

104
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:57,060
We will focus on specific risk groups and the manner in which they can be mitigated in the following lectures.

105
00:10:57,060 --> 00:11:05,060
Thank you very much for watching this introductory video. Please stay bio safe and thank you.
